      "lore": {
        "1": "A little unlabeled bottle the gas-station auntie swears is cough syrup. Nobody asks what's inside. It works.",
        "2": "Blue water in a reused bottle. Legend says it's from the same tap Old Nego washed his face at before a fight.",
        "3": "Rolled in corn husk, thick smoke. Calms the head and leaves the guard loose — the price of any calm on the street.",
        "4": "Dented can, tastes like a battery. Whoever drinks it doesn't sleep; whoever doesn't sleep doesn't miss a turn.",
        "5": "A strip of old T-shirt torn on the spot. It's no hospital, but it holds the blood till the next corner.",
        "6": "From the corner bottle at Mr. Zé's bar. Gives courage to spare and takes judgment in equal measure.",
        "8": "A June-festival firecracker with extra powder. It pops, it stinks, and nobody sees a thing for a moment.",
        "10": "Flour with powdered guarana, the recipe of whoever trains in the alley. Sits heavy and raises the dead.",
        "11": "Lit in the corner of the Hill's church. It doesn't stop punches, but the crew believes it does — sometimes that's enough.",
        "12": "A one-real bag of candy from the trailer. Pure sugar for anyone seeing stars.",
        "13": "Bolts, springs, a bit of pipe. To anyone else it's junk; to Bigode at the metal shop it's raw material.",
        "14": "Wire yanked off a pole in the dark. Worth more than it looks, and gets you more jail time than it looks, too.",
        "15": "An old radio valve, still warm from use. Only good for someone who knows how to fix what the world threw away.",
        "20": "A scraped bottle cap stamped with a fist. Whoever keeps one says it carries the punch of a brute who fell on the Track.",
        "21": "An arcade token with a wall scratched in by pocketknife. It takes one hit like it was brick.",
        "22": "An empty lighter with a chip glued on. Nobody knows how it works. Nobody wants to be in front when it does.",
        "30": "A bag of ice from the fish stall, wrapped round a twisted ankle. Whoever was dazed starts seeing again.",
        "31": "A gauze roll bought on credit at the pharmacy. Stops the cut and the shame of bleeding in front of everyone.",
        "32": "Cloth-filter coffee, black as the small hours. A limp arm lifts on the first sip.",
        "33": "The ointment grandma rubbed on everything. Closes an open guard and leaves you smelling like home.",
        "34": "A glass bottle of root, honey and prayer. Grandma never told the recipe, and nobody dared ask twice.",
        "35": "A bucket from the laundry sink thrown in the face of whoever blacked out. Rude, effective, very street.",
        "36": "Hot milk with cinnamon, to wash a hex out of the body. Grandma said spells don't like milk.",
        "37": "A glass of water with three spoons of sugar. The universal cure for anyone groggy from a scare or a punch.",
        "38": "A pharmacy patch that heats like crazy. Unlocks a stuck joint, and a stuck curse word too.",
        "39": "An aloe leaf from the rooftop pot. Cut, squeeze, spread. The burn fades; the scar stays to tell the story.",
        "201": "Ripped off mom's broom in the middle of an argument. She still wants it back.",
        "202": "Flat brim, sticker still on. On the Track, whoever peels the sticker off didn't buy it.",
        "203": "Tore in the first fight, stayed on in the second out of pride. Now it's a uniform.",
        "204": "A wrap round the fist like a garage-gym boxer. Doesn't protect much, but it commands respect.",
        "205": "Hole at the big toe, sole peeling off. Outran a lot of cops and never got caught.",
        "206": "Gold-painted tin chain from a street vendor. Shines from afar, rusts up close.",
        "207": "Real brass knuckles, turned on the lathe of someone who wouldn't give a name. Leaves marks that don't fade.",
        "208": "Black cloth with the crew's symbol. Whoever wears it never walks alone, even when they do.",
        "209": "Genuine leather, smells like an old motorbike. They say it belonged to a courier who vanished at the Market.",
        "210": "A tennis wristband from a tournament nobody played. Steadies the wrist so the punch comes out faster.",
        "211": "Army boot from a thrift store. It's kicked in more doors than its owner remembers.",
        "212": "A real gold tooth — or close enough. A smile that warns: this one's been beaten and came back.",
        "213": "A stub of construction pipe, short enough to hide in your pants. It's not a weapon, it's an argument.",
        "214": "A faded fleece beanie. Keeps the head warm and hides the face at the right moment.",
        "215": "A safety vest with magazines stuffed inside. Neighborhood engineering.",
        "216": "A bricklayer's leather glove. It's held bricks, sledgehammers and the collar of plenty of loudmouths.",
        "217": "Flip-flop with a tire sole sewn on. Won't slip even on a wet market floor.",
        "218": "A saint's medal passed from father to son. Protects the body — and the family name.",
        "219": "A manhole cover pried off a Market street. Heavy, ugly, and nothing gets past it.",
        "220": "A yellow hard hat from a site stalled for ten years. The building never went up, but the hat took plenty of blows.",
        "221": "Metal plates wired onto an old vest. Noisy, but no knife gets through.",
        "222": "A strip of truck tire strapped to the forearm. Hits it and bounces back.",
        "223": "Factory boot with a steel toe. Steps on nails, steps on people, feels nothing.",
        "224": "A wooden-bead rosary, worn smooth by prayer. Grandma prayed for you before you were born.",
        "225": "A black candle only lit at crossroads. Whoever snuffs it early pays for it later.",
        "226": "A threadbare hood that's hidden three generations of folk healers. It keeps more secrets than the head inside it.",
        "227": "Printed cloth bought at the last stall of the Market. The seller didn't charge, just said: \"it chose you\".",
        "228": "Beads of every color, one for each saint. It snapped once and its owner didn't leave home for a month.",
        "229": "A raw-leather sandal, handmade. Steps light, leaves no one's footprints.",
        "230": "A bead necklace washed in waterfall water. Whoever wears it hears a little more than everyone else.",
        "231": "A guava branch from a prayer-woman's backyard. Looks silly until someone takes a hit from it.",
        "232": "A turban tied the old way. Keeps your thoughts in place when the fight gets ugly.",
        "233": "A raw-cloth mantle embroidered in red thread. They say whoever wears it feels the street breathing.",
        "234": "A ring carved from coconut shell. When it warms on your finger, it's a warning: envy's close by.",
        "235": "A flip-flop that went through seven blessings. It won't step on an offering — it swerves on its own.",
        "236": "A blue glass eye from far away. It looks back at whoever gives you the evil eye.",
        "237": "Knuckles made from a crushed paint can. Every kid's first weapon, the day they thought they were grown.",
        "238": "A cap found at the bus stop. No owner, no brand, no shame.",
        "138": "The club the Collector used to settle accounts at the Market. Every notch in the wood is a debt someone paid with their teeth.",
        "139": "The machete Coal wore at his hip and never needed to draw. When you draw it, the whole Track goes quiet.",
        "16": "Poured through Dona Cida's cloth filter, strong as a punch. Wakes up even someone who's been asleep for years.",
        "140": "The skewer Gut-Stabber never let go of, not even asleep. Whoever holds it feels the weight of the whole Lowside.",
        "301": "Pulled off a rooftop grill in the middle of Sunday lunch. Still smells like charcoal.",
        "302": "A black hood for someone who doesn't want to be remembered. In the Lowside, vanishing is a talent.",
        "303": "A leather vest studded with old belt rivets. Each stud is from a fight its owner won.",
        "304": "A glove from a gym that shut down. The padding's gone, the anger stayed.",
        "305": "The brand's misspelled, but it runs just the same. Nobody in the Lowside asks where it came from.",
        "306": "Real silver — so they say. It jingles when its owner walks, and everyone knows he's arrived.",
        "307": "The fridge door that broke in '98. Doesn't keep anything cold, but it takes a punch beautifully.",
        "308": "A courier's helmet with a half-peeled Shadow sticker. It's saved more heads than a lot of doctors.",
        "309": "Chunks of truck tire sewn into a vest. Heavy, smelly, and nothing gets through.",
        "310": "PVC pipe cut and strapped to the arm. Cheap, ugly, works.",
        "311": "A work boot with the steel toe poking through. Step on a show-off's foot and the message is sent.",
        "312": "A wooden fig hand tied to a sprig of rue. Grandma said the evil eye doesn't get past it.",
        "313": "A dried, braided rue branch. Where it hits, it leaves a smell nobody forgets.",
        "314": "A straw hat that's been through three terreiros. Shades you from the sun and from the hex.",
        "315": "Flowered calico hand-sewn by the trackside healer. Whoever wears it feels the ground shake before the train.",
        "316": "Three knots, three wishes. It can only break on its own — and when it does, the wish comes true.",
        "317": "A rope sandal the healer wore until it wore out. Steps light and leaves the street silent.",
        "318": "A little leather pouch sewn shut with a prayer inside. Nobody opens it, nobody asks, everybody respects it."
      },
